<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#gladiator">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gladiator</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A gladiator was an armed combatant who fought other gladiators, wild animals and condemned criminals before audiences in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and Empire. Some were volunteers, but most were slaves, trained under harsh conditions and socially marginalised, though a gladiator who fought or died well could win admiration. The origin of the games is debated: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> places the first Roman games in 264 BCE, when three pairs fought at a funeral in the Forum Boarium. The games lasted nearly a thousand years, peaking between the 1st century BCE and the 2nd century CE, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> banned them in 399 and again in 404, at least in the West.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gladiator.jpg" alt="Gladiator" width="330" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Léon Gérôme · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-Leon_Gerome_Pollice_Verso.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
